# CrossDesk — environment template
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored — NEVER commit
# a real token, refresh token or party id fingerprint.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# The SAME jar and the SAME container image run against a local sandbox, a
# Devnet participant and a MainNet participant. Nothing is rebuilt between
# them — only the values below change. That is the point of this file.
#
# Three profiles are laid out in order:
#     A. LOCAL SANDBOX   — works today, no node operator, no secrets
#     B. DEVNET          — needs a participant (the standing blocker)
#     C. MAINNET         — needs B first, plus a real onboarding
# ===========================================================================


# ===========================================================================
# A. LOCAL SANDBOX  —  the profile that works right now
# ===========================================================================
# Start the ledger:   daml sandbox --port 6865
# Then upload the DAR and run Test:initialize (see README "Build it and test it").
# Parties come from the admin service, so LEDGER_PARTIES stays empty.

SERVER_PORT=8080
LEDGER_HOST=localhost
LEDGER_PORT=6865
LEDGER_TLS=false
# LEDGER_JWT=                       # empty: the sandbox has no auth
LEDGER_APPLICATION_ID=canton-dvp-desk
LEDGER_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10

# Frontend dev proxy. Point at the WSL IP when the backend runs inside WSL
# (WSL's network boundary breaks the larger gRPC streams otherwise).
# VITE_API_TARGET=http://localhost:8080


# ===========================================================================
# B. DEVNET  —  fill these in the day a participant exists
# ===========================================================================
# 🔴 STANDING BLOCKER: none of this can be tested without a participant node on
# the Global Synchronizer. Getting one is task 2 in docs/STATUS_AND_ROADMAP.md,
# and nothing downstream starts before it.
#
# LEDGER_HOST=participant.example
# LEDGER_PORT=6865
# LEDGER_TLS=true
# LEDGER_JWT=<bearer token scoping actAs / readAs>
#
# ⚠️ On a real participant the `applicationId` MUST equal the JWT `sub` claim, or
# every command is rejected. Set them together, never one without the other:
# LEDGER_APPLICATION_ID=<the JWT sub>

# --- Party roster ---------------------------------------------------------
# The 3.x bindings drop the party-management admin service, so on a shared node
# (parties pre-allocated and fixed) the roster is supplied here as
# comma-separated "Label=partyId" pairs rather than queried from the ledger.
# $SUFFIX is the participant's namespace fingerprint (`::1220…`).
#
# 🔴 Venue and Auditor are REQUIRED, not optional. resolveParty("Venue") appears at 26
# call sites (every auction and settlement path) and Auditor at 10; resolveParty THROWS
# "no known party matches" when one is absent. Test:initialize allocates them on a
# sandbox, so this only bites on a shared node.
#
# LEDGER_PARTIES=Issuer=issuer-crossdesk::$SUFFIX,Venue=venue-crossdesk::$SUFFIX,Bank=bank-crossdesk::$SUFFIX,Alice=alice-crossdesk::$SUFFIX,Bob=bob-crossdesk::$SUFFIX,Auditor=auditor-crossdesk::$SUFFIX
#
# `backend-devnet/run-devnet.sh` builds this line from $SUFFIX for you.

# --- Hosted-demo token auto-refresh (Keycloak) ----------------------------
# Leave blank for local or static-token use. When set, the backend swaps in a
# fresh access token on a schedule so a long-running deployment stays connected
# instead of silently degrading to DEGRADED when the token expires.
#
# LEDGER_TOKEN_ENDPOINT=
# LEDGER_CLIENT_ID=
# LEDGER_REFRESH_TOKEN=
# LEDGER_REFRESH_SECONDS=1800


# ===========================================================================
# C. CIP-56 REGISTRY  —  consuming somebody else's asset
# ===========================================================================
# This is the section that onboards a real ecosystem asset. There is NO
# per-asset code: the accept path reads each offer's registrar from
# `instrumentId.admin`, so a new asset is configuration, not a release.
#
# For each foreign asset you need FOUR FACTS from its issuer. They cannot be
# found — they have to be asked for. See docs/ASSET_ONBOARDING.md §1.
#
#     1. registrar party id, full, with the ::1220… fingerprint
#     2. instrument id
#     3. registry base URL
#     4. confirmation the asset implements HoldingV1
#
# Plus, where relevant: their Token Standard V2 / allocations timeline.

# Pin the instrument-admin party THIS desk's registry serves. Leave blank to
# discover it from the live TokenStandardRegistry contract. On devnet that
# discovery read is scoped to LEDGER_PARTIES, so keep the roster current.
# REGISTRY_ADMIN=

# Default foreign registry root, used for any admin without an explicit
# override below.
# REGISTRY_REMOTE_URL=

# --- Per-asset overrides: comma-separated  admin=url  pairs ----------------
# `admin` may be a full party id or its label.
#
#   cBTC — BitSafe (DLC-Link, Inc. dba BitSafe)
#   ✅ Known working. 4.16 real cBTC was claimed through this flow unaided.
#      NOTE: this is a SEPARATE holding on BitSafe's templates. The demo fund's
#      own CBTC leg is self-issued by issuer-crossdesk. Never conflate the two.
#
#   cETH — onRails  (contact: info@onrails.finance)
#   ⏳ BLOCKED ON THE FOUR FACTS. onRails' integration page states cETH is a
#      CIP-0056 asset, so this flow SHOULD apply unchanged with a different
#      registrar — an expectation, not a verified fact. Procedure:
#      docs/TOKEN_STANDARD_RUNBOOK.md §3.
#      ⚠️ cETH is onRails', NOT BitSafe's.
#
# REGISTRY_REMOTE_URLS=bitsafe=https://<their-registry>,onrails=https://<their-registry>

# Prefer the token-standard interface package this backend was compiled
# against when resolving an interface exercise. Turn OFF only if a participant
# cannot resolve that exact package.
REGISTRY_PIN_INTERFACE_PACKAGE=true


# ===========================================================================
# D. MAINNET  —  what changes, and what does not
# ===========================================================================
# Identical shape to B. The differences are operational, not configural:
#
#   - a MainNet participant and Global Synchronizer onboarding (not obtainable
#     from code — needs an onboarding secret or a sponsor);
#   - real party ids with a different ::1220… fingerprint;
#   - real registrar URLs in REGISTRY_REMOTE_URLS;
#   - secrets injected from a k8s Secret or a vault, never from a file on disk;
#   - LEDGER_TLS=true, always.
#
# Before pointing anything at MainNet, work docs/PRODUCTION_CHECKLIST.md.
#
# 🔴 And note what MainNet does NOT change: publishing a number that contracts
# reference is benchmark administration, which is a regulated activity in most
# jurisdictions. LICENSE §7 states that nothing here is a price, a valuation or
# a benchmark. Keep that true until there is an entity and, where required, an
# authorisation.


# ===========================================================================
# E. DECENTRALIZED PARTY (BitSafe Decentralization Manager)
# ===========================================================================
# 🔴 NOT IMPLEMENTED. Placeholders so the wiring is obvious the day a node exists.
#
# The venue operator is currently ONE party holding ONE key. The honest answer to
# "what stops you re-running the close in your favour?" is nothing technical. The fix
# is to make that party a Decentralized Party: same `hint::fingerprint` id, but the
# key behind it split across independent operators, so no one of them can act alone
# and the customer can be one of the signers.
#
# The Daml does not change. `operator = <a decentralized party>` type-checks and
# behaves identically, because nothing in it inspects how a key is hosted. What
# changes is HOW THE BACKEND SUBMITS - see the DECENTRALIZED-PARTY SEAM comment in
# LedgerService.submit(). Affected writes are the operator/administrator-controlled
# ones only: CloseBidding, RunClose, ProcessCreation, ProcessRedemption.
# NOT FinalizeFixing - that is `controller proposer` (Governance.daml:542), a committee
# member, not the operator. A close is TWO operator submissions, not one.
#
# Run Decentralization Manager ALONGSIDE the participant. Do not vendor it.
#
# DPM_COORDINATOR_URL=          # where the coordinator gathers signatures
# DPM_OPERATOR_PARTY=           # the decentralized party id, hint::1220...
# DPM_THRESHOLD=                # e.g. 3 of 5 key holders
# DPM_ENABLED=false             # keep false until the latency experiment is done
#
# 🔴 OPEN QUESTION, and the reason this is not switched on: a threshold-coordinated
# write is slower than the ~7.5s observed on a shared participant, and a fixing has a
# strike time. FIXING_METHODOLOGY.md §4 defines the strike as the moment quorum is
# reached, NOT the commit - so latency does not move the strike, but it does bound how
# late publication can be. Measure before enabling.
